WEBVTT

1
00:00:02.700 --> 00:00:07.446
Your friend's café did well, and now she wants three, in three parts of town.

2
00:00:07.446 --> 00:00:12.377
So you copy the drawing three times, and change the street on each copy by hand.

3
00:00:12.600 --> 00:00:14.426
On the third copy you forget.

4
00:00:14.426 --> 00:00:17.008
The builder turns up at the wrong street.

5
00:00:17.008 --> 00:00:18.330
The drawing was fine.

6
00:00:18.330 --> 00:00:20.093
The copying was the problem.

7
00:00:20.900 --> 00:00:24.021
What we want is one drawing and a list of names.

8
00:00:24.021 --> 00:00:28.572
Terraform has two ways to make one copy per name: count, and for_each.

9
00:00:28.800 --> 00:00:32.181
And here is the question that sounds too simple to ask.

10
00:00:32.181 --> 00:00:33.595
Both make three copies.

11
00:00:33.595 --> 00:00:36.116
Does it really matter which one you pick?

12
00:00:36.733 --> 00:00:41.205
Picture a builder making garden sheds for three families on that list.

13
00:00:41.205 --> 00:00:44.909
This builder numbers the sheds by their place on the list.

14
00:00:44.909 --> 00:00:48.423
Alpha gets shed zero, bravo shed one, charlie shed two.

15
00:00:48.633 --> 00:00:49.641
That is count.

16
00:00:49.641 --> 00:00:57.344
count equals three makes local_file.by_count zero, one and two, and count.index tells each copy its number.

17
00:00:57.344 --> 00:01:01.304
Each one is a small file that says hello to its family.

18
00:01:01.533 --> 00:01:02.842
Now alpha cancels.

19
00:01:02.842 --> 00:01:05.749
We remove alpha from the list, and plan.

20
00:01:05.967 --> 00:01:08.128
How many sheds does the builder touch?

21
00:01:10.767 --> 00:01:12.446
The obvious answer is one.

22
00:01:12.446 --> 00:01:15.546
Knock down alpha's shed, leave the others alone.

23
00:01:15.546 --> 00:01:17.677
Let's see if that is really true.

24
00:01:18.200 --> 00:01:20.320
The builder reads the new list.

25
00:01:20.320 --> 00:01:23.467
Bravo is first now, so bravo gets number zero.

26
00:01:23.467 --> 00:01:26.135
Charlie is second, so charlie gets one.

27
00:01:26.367 --> 00:01:29.631
So shed zero is torn down and rebuilt for bravo.

28
00:01:29.631 --> 00:01:32.759
Shed one is torn down and rebuilt for charlie.

29
00:01:32.967 --> 00:01:37.272
And shed two has nobody left on the list, so it is knocked down.

30
00:01:37.272 --> 00:01:38.617
One cancelled order.

31
00:01:38.617 --> 00:01:39.963
Three sheds touched.

32
00:01:40.367 --> 00:01:40.887
Well...

33
00:01:40.887 --> 00:01:45.649
bravo and charlie just got brand new sheds they never asked for.

34
00:01:45.867 --> 00:01:48.183
The real plan says the same thing.

35
00:01:48.183 --> 00:01:54.042
Copy zero's content changes from hello alpha to hello bravo, which forces replacement.

36
00:01:54.042 --> 00:01:58.879
And copy two is destroyed, because index two is out of range for count.

37
00:01:59.100 --> 00:02:01.234
Now the second builder, for_each.

38
00:02:01.234 --> 00:02:05.114
Same list, but this builder nails a name card to every shed.

39
00:02:05.114 --> 00:02:09.381
The address is the name: local_file.by_each alpha, bravo, charlie.

40
00:02:09.600 --> 00:02:10.790
Alpha cancels again.

41
00:02:10.790 --> 00:02:15.195
The builder looks for the card that says alpha, and knocks down that shed.

42
00:02:15.195 --> 00:02:19.540
Bravo's and charlie's sheds keep their cards, so nothing happens to them.

43
00:02:20.067 --> 00:02:25.761
The lab's practice folder does both at once, so here is its real summary line.

44
00:02:25.761 --> 00:02:28.827
Plan: 2 to add, 0 to change, 4 to destroy.

45
00:02:29.033 --> 00:02:33.197
Three of those four destroys come from count, for one removed name.

46
00:02:33.197 --> 00:02:34.999
Only one comes from for_each.

47
00:02:35.600 --> 00:02:38.119
So here is the whole idea in one line.

48
00:02:38.119 --> 00:02:40.307
A copy's address is its identity.

49
00:02:40.307 --> 00:02:44.285
With count, the identity is a position, and positions shift.

50
00:02:44.285 --> 00:02:47.467
With for_each, it is a name, and names stay put.

51
00:02:48.100 --> 00:02:49.688
So when is count fine?

52
00:02:49.688 --> 00:02:56.327
For nearly identical copies, or as an on-off switch: count equals one if enabled, else zero.

53
00:02:56.533 --> 00:03:00.350
Use for_each when each copy has its own name or settings.

54
00:03:00.350 --> 00:03:01.957
It takes a map or a set.

55
00:03:01.957 --> 00:03:07.113
A plain list is rejected with Invalid for_each argument, so wrap it in toset.

56
00:03:07.333 --> 00:03:09.096
One limit applies to both.

57
00:03:09.096 --> 00:03:17.843
Terraform must know the count or the keys before anything exists, so they cannot come from a value that appears only after apply.

58
00:03:18.067 --> 00:03:20.035
And files are cheap to replace.

59
00:03:20.035 --> 00:03:23.399
Containers with data, or servers in a cloud, are not.

60
00:03:23.399 --> 00:03:26.129
That is when a shifted number really hurts.

61
00:03:26.733 --> 00:03:30.670
The moral of the story: give named things names, not numbers.

62
00:03:30.670 --> 00:03:33.381
Then one cancelled order touches one shed.

63
00:03:34.000 --> 00:03:34.975
A quick check.

64
00:03:34.975 --> 00:03:37.829
In the for_each sheds, bravo cancels too.

65
00:03:37.829 --> 00:03:40.335
How many copies does the plan touch?

66
00:03:43.000 --> 00:03:43.307
One.

67
00:03:43.307 --> 00:03:46.067
Only the shed whose card says bravo.

68
00:03:46.067 --> 00:03:48.673
Charlie's card still says charlie.
